            <h2 id="sharing-mechanics-heading" class="mt-2 text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-4xl">
                How One-Time Share Links Keep Keys Out of Server Logs
            </h2>
            <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                When you share a credential in PassKee, your browser generates a dedicated one-time key and places it after the URL hash symbol (<code class="rounded bg-slate-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-emerald-300">#</code>). Under RFC 3986 HTTP standards, browsers never transmit the URL fragment to the web server.
            </p>
